## src/lib.zag — sibling-tls library root.
##
## Trivial stub for the worked-example path-dep. Real TLS implementation
## is unrelated to the fixture role -- this sibling exists so that
## `internal-tls = { path = "../sibling-tls" }` in
## `examples/project_layout/zag.toml` resolves to a real .zag file under
## the on-disk tree, and so the `examples/run_all.sh` harness finds a
## valid runnable program if it walks into this directory.
##
## The `fun main()` below is a harness-friendly stub: `examples/run_all.sh`
## runs `zag run <file>` on every file it finds; this sibling's
## production entry point is to be invoked via the path-dep resolver,
## which is not yet wired. Until that lands, we expose a no-op main() so
## the harness stays green if it walks into this directory.
##
## ASYMMETRY: hard-coded "ok" instead of `{tls_marker()}`. Both Display
## branches are wired in today's compiler (per docs/spec.md §4.5, bool
## renders as `true`/`false` like every primitive); the asymmetry is a
## legibility choice, not a Display gap. `lib_marker` interpolates
## because showing the integer value is the demo; `tls_marker` hard-codes
## "ok" because in a fixture status line, "true"/"false" is less readable
## than an explicit confirm. Compare [`examples/project_layout/src/lib.zag`](../project_layout/src/lib.zag) for the integer branch.
pub fun tls_marker() -> bool {
    return true;
}

fun main() {
    print("tls_marker ok\n");
}
